00:05:02.914 --> 00:05:04.620
- [Chuck] Knagor Drak wants to know

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.613
what's the most realistic
way that space could kill us?

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:11.900
- Ooh, I like that, okay.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:12.980
If the sun

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:14.920
begins to

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:15.903
misbehave,

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:19.380
we expect that to happen in
about five billion years,

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:22.040
they'll be considerably more solar storms,

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:25.230
considerably more an intense

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:28.380
radiative particles
emanating from the sun.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:32.480
As that happens, it will
begin to ablate our atmosphere

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.680
and, basically, taking
out the highest level

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:39.540
layers of the atmosphere systematically.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:40.800
And as it continues to do this,

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:42.383
atmospheric pressure will drop.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:45.420
We'll have less and less air to breathe

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.420
as this process continues and, eventually,

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.630
we'll have no air to breathe at all.

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:55.590
And unless we become fish
(laughing) real fast,

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:57.200
we get to breathe the remaining air

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.290
that's dissolved in the ocean,
but that'll go away too.

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:02.420
All the fishes will die.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:05.500
And, when that happens,

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:06.790
we all suffocate.

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:11.110
That's space killing
us in a diabolical way.

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:13.720
- And it's space finally making Aquaman

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.645
the preeminent superhero.

00:06:15.645 --> 00:06:17.250
(Neil laughs)

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.140
- Chuck, you know another
way for space to kill us?

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:20.060
- What?

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:21.640
- So, if there's a gamma ray burst

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:23.600
that happens a little too close?

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.070
What'll happen is the
high energy radiation

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:28.770
hitting Earth will first
hit the ozone layer,

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:30.890
that's our first line of
protection right there.

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:33.810
Ozone takes out the x-rays
and the ultraviolet,

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:35.460
but then, there's so much of it

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:38.640
the ozone layer will not
have a chance to recover.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:41.960
And so, it's just waves
and waves of radiation

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:46.090
until the entire ozone layer is gone.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:48.680
And the next wave of that
high energy radiation

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:51.913
then reaches Earth, giving
everyone skin cancer.

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:54.820
Some people, skin cancer
sooner than others.

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:57.925
- I'm pretty sure I'm
in the later category.

00:06:57.925 --> 00:06:59.473
(Neil laughs)

00:06:59.473 --> 00:07:00.830
- Oh Chuck.

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:01.890
- All right it's caption time,

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:03.220
where we took a picture of you Neil

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:05.380
and, of course, asked for captions.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:07.240
- So, can I offer my own caption?

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:08.560
- Go ahead.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:09.590
- Back up!

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:12.050
I'm about to drop some science on you!

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:13.660
- All right, well let's
see how that stacks up

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:17.277
